Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 23:57:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05738/11362] [outer-loop] fix real duration_s=0.0 bug in the
 .480 capstone artifact

Investigated the .480 capstone's DURATION_TOO_SHORT flag. Unlike the
prior four false-positive substrate-recognition bugs this session, this
one is a genuine defect: the artifact's own inference_substrate
(cached_fixture_replay_no_llm) was already correctly recognized by the
linter, but duration_s was literally 0.0, not just implausibly short.

Root cause: build_artifact()'s duration_s parameter had a dangerous
`float = 0.0` default. The script has no CLI entrypoint or main() -- it's
invoked directly by whatever runner drove the real .480 task -- and that
invocation never measured wall-clock time and passed it in, silently
falling back to the default. The existing test suite already exercises
duration_s=1.0 explicitly in every test, so this was purely a real-run
invocation gap, not a logic bug in build_artifact() itself.

Fix: removed the dangerous default, making duration_s a required
keyword-only parameter -- closes the footgun for any future invocation of
this module, not just a one-off patch. Confirmed no other callers exist.

Regenerated the artifact by re-invoking build_artifact() (a pure,
deterministic function over already-cached JSON files) with genuine
time.perf_counter() timing around the call, then recomputed
reproducibility_checksum to keep the artifact internally consistent with
its own validate_artifact() check. Real measured duration: 0.012s --
plausible for reading a handful of cached JSON files and computing a
checksum, matching the substrate's own 0.0001s floor with real headroom.

Verified: adversarial_verify.py now shows 0 CRITICAL flags (was 1); the
one remaining INFO-level IMPLAUSIBLE_PERFECT (arc_level_delta=0.0) is
genuine and already explained in the artifact's own honest_verdict text
("ARC delta 0 with patch retired"), not a stub default. Full test suite:
7/7 passed (was 6/7 -- the previously-failing test was the suite's own
self-check that the deployed artifact isn't flagged_adversarial, now
correctly passing since the artifact is actually clean).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
